  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 223 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nFor most of the work week, the mid/upper-level pattern features\ntroughing across the Pacific Coast, ridging centered near the \nCentral Plains, and troughing across New England. The mid-level \nridge that will have influence across our area this week has \nbeen shifted slightly to the west over the last few model runs. \nThis has resulted in a 1-2 degree lowering of forecast \ntemperatures this week, as the flow aloft is now expected to be \nmostly northwesterly. As mentioned in previous forecast \ndiscussions, the NBM has been near the warmest piece of \nguidance, but has trended a bit cooler, likely due to the \nwestward shift of ridge placement. The LREF indicates that mean \n850mb temperatures remain near 10C into Wednesday before \nslightly warming to around 12C Thursday and into the weekend. \nAssuming full mixing, which appears reasonable considering \nabundant sunshine, this would lead to highs in the low/mid 70s \nthrough Wednesday with some locations reaching the upper 70s to \nnear 80 degrees after Thursday. In addition, LREF temperature \nIQRs indicate higher confidence as IQR spreads are only around \n3-5 degrees for the entire week. There is a weak signal that \nlongwave troughing nudges eastward towards the region next \nweekend, with a wide range of potential solutions leading to a \nlow confidence forecast by that timeframe. The general consensus\nis that mid-level ridging maintains influence across the area \nthrough most of next weekend, resulting in a continuation of dry\nand seasonably warm conditions.",
